{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"files\":[{\"content\":\"#include<stdio.h>\\n#include<stdlib.h>\\n#include<string.h>\\n\\n/* Maximum size (in char) for neighbors and compute lists */\\n#define MAX 100\\n#define PARALLEL_THRESHOLD 256\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n  int id;\\n  float value;\\n  char neighbors[MAX];\\n} Node;\\n\\n\\n/* Reads input, allocates memory, writes graph size and node list. Returns the graph. */\\nNode* read_input(char* fname, int *n, char* list) {\\n  Node* data = NULL;\\n  FILE *fl = NULL;\\n\\n  if (fname == NULL)\\n    fl = stdin;\\n  else\\n    fl = fopen(fname, \\\"r\\\");\\n\\n  if (fl == NULL) {\\n    fprintf(stderr, \\\"Error!\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n  }\\n\\n  /* header */\\n  fscanf(fl, \\\"%d %[^\\n]\\\", n, list);\\n\\n  if (*n > 0) {\\n    data = (Node*) malloc ((*n)*sizeof(Node));\\n    if(data == NULL){\\n      fprintf(stderr, \\\"Error!\\\\n\\\");\\n      exit(1);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  for(int i = 0; i < *n; i++)\\n    fscanf(fl, \\\"%d %f %[^\\n]\\\", &data[i].id, &data[i].value, data[i].neighbors);\\n\\n  fclose(fl);\\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\n/* Returns the index of the node with the given id or -1 if not found */\\nint find_by_id(Node g[], int id, int size){\\n  int i = 0;\\n  while((i < size) && g[i].id != id)\\n    i++;\\n  if(i < size)\\n    return i;\\n  return -1;\\n}\\n\\n/* Main program - calls read input, computes sums, prints output */\\nint main() {\\n  char *fileN = NULL;\\n  char computelist[MAX];\\n  int n = -1;\\n  Node* input = NULL;\\n  int *successor = NULL;\\n  int *pred_count = NULL;\\n  int *offset = NULL;\\n  int *cursor = NULL;\\n  int *predecessors = NULL;\\n  int *frontier = NULL;\\n  int *next_frontier = NULL;\\n  float *cost = NULL;\\n\\n  input = read_input(fileN, &n, computelist);\\n\\n  if (n > 0) {\\n    successor = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));\\n    pred_count = (int*) calloc(n, sizeof(int));\\n    offset = (int*) malloc((n + 1) * sizeof(int));\\n    cursor = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));\\n    predecessors = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));\\n    frontier = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));\\n    next_frontier = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));\\n    cost = (float*) malloc(n * sizeof(float));\\n\\n    if (successor == NULL || pred_count == NULL || offset == NULL ||\\n        cursor == NULL || predecessors == NULL || frontier == NULL ||\\n        next_frontier == NULL || cost == NULL) {\\n      fprintf(stderr, \\\"Error!\\\\n\\\");\\n      exit(1);\\n    }\\n\\n    /* Select the same single best neighbor used by get_costly for each node. */\\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) if(n > PARALLEL_THRESHOLD)\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      char aux[MAX];\\n      char *auxptr;\\n      char *t;\\n      int best_id = -1;\\n      int best_index = -1;\\n      float max = 0;\\n\\n      strcpy(aux, input[i].neighbors);\\n      t = strtok_r(aux, \\\" \\\", &auxptr);\\n      while (t != NULL) {\\n        int id = atoi(t);\\n        int d = find_by_id(input, id, n);\\n        if (d >= 0 && max < input[d].value) {\\n          max = input[d].value;\\n          best_id = id;\\n          best_index = d;\\n        }\\n        t = strtok_r(NULL, \\\" \\\", &auxptr);\\n      }\\n\\n      /* The original routine uses -1 as its no-successor sentinel. */\\n      successor[i] = (best_id != -1) ? best_index : -1;\\n    }\\n\\n    /* Build reverse edges for the selected-successor graph. */\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\\n      if (successor[i] >= 0)\\n        pred_count[successor[i]]++;\\n\\n    offset[0] = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\\n      offset[i + 1] = offset[i] + pred_count[i];\\n\\n    memcpy(cursor, offset, n * sizeof(int));\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\\n      if (successor[i] >= 0)\\n        predecessors[cursor[successor[i]]++] = i;\\n\\n    /* Evaluate sink-to-source layers; every selected edge is in the input DAG. */\\n    int frontier_count = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\\n      if (successor[i] < 0)\\n        frontier[frontier_count++] = i;\\n\\n    int next_count = 0;\\n    #pragma omp parallel if(n > PARALLEL_THRESHOLD) shared(frontier, next_frontier, frontier_count, next_count, cost, successor, offset, predecessors)\\n    {\\n      while (frontier_count > 0) {\\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n        for (int j = 0; j < frontier_count; j++) {\\n          int i = frontier[j];\\n          float r = input[i].value;\\n          if (successor[i] >= 0)\\n            r += cost[successor[i]];\\n          cost[i] = r;\\n        }\\n\\n        #pragma omp single\\n        next_count = 0;\\n\\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n        for (int j = 0; j < frontier_count; j++) {\\n          int i = frontier[j];\\n          for (int p = offset[i]; p < offset[i + 1]; p++) {\\n            int slot;\\n            #pragma omp atomic capture\\n            slot = next_count++;\\n            next_frontier[slot] = predecessors[p];\\n          }\\n        }\\n\\n        #pragma omp single\\n        {\\n          int *tmp = frontier;\\n          frontier = next_frontier;\\n          next_frontier = tmp;\\n          frontier_count = next_count;\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  /* Reuse the precomputed result for every requested ID, including repeats. */\\n  for(char *c = strtok(computelist, \\\" \\\"); c != NULL; c = strtok(NULL, \\\" \\\")) {\\n    int nd = find_by_id(input, atoi(c), n);\\n    float result = (nd >= 0) ? cost[nd] : 0.0f;\\n    printf(\\\"%s: %f\\\\n\\\", c, result);\\n  }\\n\\n  free(input);\\n  free(successor);\\n  free(pred_count);\\n  free(offset);\\n  free(cursor);\\n  free(predecessors);\\n  free(frontier);\\n  free(next_frontier);\\n  free(cost);\\n\\n  return 0;\\n}\\n\",\"path\":\"src/graph.c\"}],\"hypothesis\":\"Precomputing each node’s chosen successor once and evaluating the resulting DAG from sinks toward sources can avoid repeated recursive traversal. OpenMP work sharing should help on larger graphs, while a size threshold avoids parallel startup for small inputs.\",\"notes\":\"The selected successor preserves the original direct-neighbor value comparison, neighbor order for ties, missing-ID behavior, and the -1 sentinel. Each node’s value is added to its successor’s completed float result in the same chain order. Correctness assumes the stated input DAG; no measurements were run.\",\"research\":{\"queries\":[],\"urls\":[]},\"sources_kept\":[],\"transformations\":[\"Replace repeated recursive get_costly calls with a single per-node successor selection and reverse-edge construction.\",\"Evaluate selected-successor dependencies in sink-to-source frontiers, preserving per-node float addition order.\",\"Use OpenMP work sharing for selection and frontier processing only when n exceeds 256; statically schedule the independent loops.\"]}"}}
